Source-encoding adjudication probes, 2026-09-15. Not an implementation change.
Image evo-flight-lab:sealed, linux/arm64, network none.
GCC-14 Debian 14.2.0-19; Clang-19 Debian 19.1.7.
Input is supplied byte-for-byte on stdin, not decoded as UTF-8 first.

Notation <FF> below means one raw byte 0xff, NOT those four ASCII characters:
string:     int main(void){return (unsigned char)"<FF>"[0] != 255;}
character:  int main(void){return (unsigned char)'<FF>' != 255;}
comment:    /* <FF> */ int main(void){return 0;}
identifier: int <FF>; int main(void){return 0;}

For each case/CC/LEVEL:
  raw-input | CC -std=gnu11 -OLEVEL -x c - -o /tmp/source-encoding-probe
  if compilation exits 0: /tmp/source-encoding-probe
Example exact input generator for the string case (shell printf):
  printf 'int main(void){return (unsigned char)"\377"[0] != 255;}\n'

case        gcc-O0 gcc-O2 clang-O0 clang-O2 (compile/run exit codes)
string      0/0    0/0    0/0      0/0
character   0/0    0/0    0/0      0/0
comment     0/0    0/0    0/0      0/0
identifier  1/-    1/-    1/-      1/-

GCC identifier: error: stray '\377' in program.
Clang identifier: error: source file is not valid UTF-8.
Clang literal cases additionally warn: illegal character encoding in
string/character literal [-Winvalid-source-encoding]. Warnings were retained.
This measures the byte in these four lexical locations, not arbitrary source
charset conversion, wide literals, macro stringization or include filenames.
The approved revision 1.9.102 distinguishes raw literal bytes from Unicode
source characters and preserves E0002 for unsupported positions. The new
source_bytes lexer/CLI tests exercise shared main/stdin/include behavior;
the historical observations above are not relabeled as a new campaign.
